<h2>The statutory foundations</h2>
<p>Three regimes converge on the same answer. The secular, summary remedy is Section 144 BNSS: a Magistrate of the first class may order monthly maintenance where a person having sufficient means neglects or refuses to maintain, among others, a legitimate <em>or illegitimate</em> minor child unable to maintain itself — and an adult child so disabled. The personal-law route for Hindus is Section 20 of the Hindu Adoptions and Maintenance Act, 1956, which binds a Hindu, male or female, to maintain legitimate and illegitimate children during minority, with a daughter&rsquo;s claim extending until marriage where she cannot maintain herself. And where the facts disclose domestic violence within a domestic relationship, monetary relief under Section 20 of the DV Act can carry the child&rsquo;s expenses as well.</p>
<table class="law">
